ow someone to Chi if they have the necessary tiles. For example, if the previous player has a Chow in W4-W5-W6, they might need W5. But I don't have W4 or W6. So no. Similarly for T5.\n\nTherefore, the safest discard is B4, as it's not part of any potential meld and doesn't help others. So the action would be Play B4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has three melds (Chow of W1-W2-W3, Chow of B7-B8-B9, Pung of B2s) and a pair of F2s. The remaining tiles (W5, T5, B4) cannot form an additional meld. To complete a winning hand, a fourth meld is needed. Discarding B4 is optimal as it is a middle tile with no potential for forming a Chow or Pung, and it minimizes the risk of aiding opponents.  \nReason: Discarding B4 preserves the pair of F2s and avoids creating opportunities for opponents to form melds. This maintains the possibility of completing a fourth meld with the remaining tiles (W5, T5) if a suitable tile is drawn later.  \nAnswer: Play B4"}
