Dots), so All Types applies. So that would be 6 + 2 = 8 points. That meets the minimum requirement. \n\nIf the user forms a pair of T8, then the hand is four Chows and a pair of T8. Same as above, All Chows (2 points) and All Types (6 points), total 8 points. \n\nBut wait, the user's melds include a Pung of B2, which is a Pung of a suited tile. So the hand would have a Pung and three Chows. That would also count as All Pungs? No, All Pungs requires four Pungs. So no. The hand has one Pung and three Chows. So the scoring is All Chows (2 points) and All Types (6 points). \n\nBut there's also the possibility of other patterns. For example, if the pair is a terminal tile (like T8 is a terminal in the Dots suit), then Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point). So if the pair is T8 (which is a terminal), then that adds 1 point. So total would be 2 + 6 + 1 = 9 points. \n\nIf the pair is W5, which is a middle tile, then no additional points from that. So total would be 8 points. \n\nTherefore, discarding T8 to form a pair of T8 would give a slightly higher score (9 points) compared to discarding W5 (8 points). So the user would prefer to discard T8 to get the extra point from the Pung of Terminals. \n\nAdditionally, considering the number of remaining tiles: T8 has two left, W5 has three. But since the user is going for the higher score, it's better to go for the 9-point option. \n\nTherefore, the better move is to discard T8, so that the user can form a pair of T8, which is a terminal tile, adding an extra point. \n\nSo the valid action would be Play T8.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding T8 allows forming a pair of terminal tiles (T8), adding an extra scoring point (Pung of Terminals or Honors) compared to discarding W5. This results in a higher total score (9 vs. 8 points) while maintaining a safe discard given the unshown tile counts.  \nReason: Forming a pair of T8 (a terminal tile) adds 1 point for Pung of Terminals/Honors, increasing the total score to 9 points. T8 ha